It calls the cloud service behind the AldesConnect app
(https://aldesiotsuite-aldeswebapi.azurewebsites.net) with the e-mail and the
password of the user's AldesConnect account, the way the app does. Aldes
publishes no documentation for it. Aldes, AldesConnect, InspirAIR, EasyHOME,
Dee Fly and T.One are trademarks of Aldes; this integration is not affiliated
with nor endorsed by Aldes.

Protocol knowledge
------------------
The routes, the commands, the fields of the products and their meaning (mode
letters, tenths of degrees, holiday command, setpoint bounds) come from these
community projects, read but not copied:
  - homeassistant-aldes by guix77 (MIT),
    https://github.com/guix77/homeassistant-aldes;
  - homeassistant-aldes by saniho (MIT),
    https://github.com/saniho/homeassistant-aldes, and aldes-bridge (MIT),
    https://github.com/saniho/aldes-bridge (mode tables of the T.One);
  - aldes-homebridge-plugin by jp-gouin (Apache-2.0),
    https://github.com/jp-gouin/aldes-homebridge-plugin;
  - homebridge-aldesVMC by jakatt (MIT),
    https://github.com/jakatt/homebridge-aldesVMC;
  - aldes2mqtt by Smeagolworms4, https://github.com/Smeagolworms4/aldes2mqtt;
  - aldes-mqtt-bridge (MIT), https://github.com/djo1338/aldes-mqtt-bridge
    (the command formats, docs/PROTOCOL.md);
  - hassio_aldes by aalmazanarbs, https://github.com/aalmazanarbs/hassio_aldes.
Only facts about the service were taken from them (endpoints, field names,
codes), never code. The answers to wrong credentials and to a missing or
refused token were checked against the service itself. This integration is an
independent JavaScript implementation.
